How Does It Work?

In a multiprocessor system:

Multiple CPUs are connected to the same computer system.
They can access shared memory and I/O devices.
A task can be divided into smaller parts.
Different processors execute different parts simultaneously.
The results are combined to complete the overall task.

For example, if a large task has four independent parts:

             Large Task
                 │
       ┌─────────┼─────────┐
       ↓         ↓         ↓
     CPU 1     CPU 2     CPU 3     CPU 4
       │         │         │         │
       └─────────┴─────────┴─────────┘
                    ↓
              Final Result
Types of Multiprocessor Systems
1. Symmetric Multiprocessing (SMP)

In SMP, all processors have equal status and can perform operating-system and application tasks.

CPU 1 ──┐
CPU 2 ──┤
CPU 3 ──┼──→ Shared Memory
CPU 4 ──┘

Features:

All processors are treated equally.
Shared memory is used.
Better workload distribution.
2. Asymmetric Multiprocessing (AMP)

In AMP, processors have different roles.

One processor may act as the master, while other processors perform specific tasks.

          Master CPU
              │
       ┌──────┼──────┐
       ↓      ↓      ↓
     CPU 2   CPU 3   CPU 4
Advantages
Higher performance — multiple processors can execute tasks simultaneously.
Increased reliability — failure of one processor may not stop the entire system.
Resource sharing — processors can share memory and I/O devices.
Parallel processing — large tasks can be divided among processors.
Better throughput — more tasks can be completed in a given period.
Disadvantages
High cost — requires multiple processors and additional hardware.
Complex design — synchronization and communication between processors are required.
Memory contention — processors may compete for access to shared memory.
Software complexity — programs need to be designed to take advantage of parallel processing.
Multiprocessor vs Multicomputer
Multiprocessor	Multicomputer
Multiple CPUs in one system	Multiple independent computers
Usually shares main memory	Each computer generally has its own memory
Tightly coupled	Loosely coupled
Communication through shared memory/system interconnect	Communication through a network/interconnect
